.TH WSYS 4
.SH NAME
wsys \- window system, prototype (processor backend), rio(4)'s files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsys
[
.B -dgpOx
] [
.B -f
.I font
]
[
.B -s
.I srvname
]
[
.B -i
.I cmd
]
[
.B -x
]
.PP
.B mount $wsys /mnt/wsys
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wsys
is the window system of the
.B ui
branch, a successor of
.IR rio (1)
in the making.
It draws on the screen
.IR draw (3)
gives it (or in the window of
.BR /dev/winname ),
posts a 9P connection as
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BI wsys.$user. pid\fR,
as
.IR rio (1)
does: one that died leaves its entry behind; then also as
.BR wsys.$user ,
replacing what was there: the user's newest
.IR wsys ,
where a program that outlived one finds the next, as
.IR wlsys (4)
does)
and serves a tree of windows.
With
.BR -i ,
it runs
.I cmd
with
.IR rc (1)
in a name space of its own with the tree on
.B /mnt/wsys
and
.B $wsys
naming the service;
.B -x
makes it exit when
.I cmd
does;
.B -O
uses no overlay plane (see
.B gpubuffer
below).
.PP
Where it can,
.I wsys
composes the screen on the GPU (Vulkan, Mesa's
.BR libvulkan.a ):
a swapchain on the whole screen goes to the display (gpu(4)
.BR scanout ),
each frame from what
.IR draw (3)
changed on the screen (gpu(3)'s
.BR dirty ,
copied from its framebuffer by the processor) and, over it, the windows'
GPU buffers where they are seen, copied by the GPU (the processor copies
none of their pixels; the window on the overlay plane, below, is left out:
the plane shows it over the frames).
A presented GPU buffer stays
.IR wsys 's
while it is shown: the client gets the one shown before back
.RB ( "b \fIn\fP" )
when a frame with the new one is on its way, at the display's rate.
.B /mnt/wsys/screen
and a GPU window's
.B window
are then read back from a frame; such a window's own image in
.IR draw (3)
does not have the client's pixels.
Only on the whole screen (not in a window), with an x8r8g8b8 screen, a
GPU's driver drawing it (gpu(3)'s framebuffer; not under VESA);
otherwise, if Vulkan does not start, when its thread fails, or when
the display refuses its frames (the WSI would copy them into the
screen they are composed from), the processor composes.
.B -p
makes the processor compose;
.B -g
asks for the GPU and says why not when it cannot, and what it does
(as does
.BR WSYSDEBUG=comp ).
.PP
With
.BR -d ,
.IR draw (3)
is
.IR wsys 's
own: the kernel's devdraw, libmemdraw and libmemlayer in its process
.RB ( drawfs (4)'s,
built for APE), on a screen as big as the kernel's in a segment of
.IR segment (3)
.RB ( wsys.screen. pid).
Its tree and every window's directory have a directory
.B draw
.RB ( new ,
.IB n /colormapR,
.BR ctl ,
.BR data ,
.BR refresh ,
as
.BR #i 's),
so the programs in its windows, which find the window's directory on
.BR /dev ,
draw through it.
What it drew the GPU composes from that segment, or
.I wsys
copies into the kernel's screen.
.PP
A window's pixels are not sent through the files: they are in global
segments of
.IR segment (3)
that the client makes and owns, its buffers.
As
.I wsys
attaches a segment at the address it has everywhere, each window gets a
range of addresses of its own
.RI ( base ,
.IR size );
the client puts its buffers there, and they cannot collide in
.IR wsys .
A buffer's layout is
.B x8r8g8b8
(32 bits a pixel, little-endian: blue, green, red, unused),
a line every
.I stride
bytes.
Once announced, the client may remove the segment's entry in
.BR #g :
the segment lives while either side has it attached.
.PP
Each window is also a window of
.IR draw (3)
on
.IR wsys 's
screen, border and all, publicly named; and
.I wsys
serves
.IR rio (4)'s
files, so that
.IR draw (3)
programs,
.IR window (1)
and
.I rio
itself run in its windows unchanged.
The attach name
.B new
with
.IR rio 's
options
.RB ( -r ,
.BR -dx ,
.BR -dy ,
.BR -pid ,
.BR -cd )
makes a window and attaches to its directory; a window's number attaches to
an existing one;
.B none
or none at all gives the tree below.
.B wctl
at the root takes
.BR new ;
a window's
.BR wctl ,
.IR rio 's
messages
.RB ( resize ,
.BR move ,
.BR top ,
.BR bottom ,
.BR current ,
.BR delete ,
.BR set ,
.BR new ).
A process
.I wsys
starts in a new window gets a name space with the window's files on
.B /dev
and
.BR /mnt/wsys ,
and a note group of its own;
.B delete
sends it
.BR hangup ,
Del in a window that is not raw
.BR interrupt .
A window lives while its files are open (or the
.B clone
file that made it).
.PP
A window made by
.B new
is a text window, as
.IR rio 's:
while no program has its
.B mouse
open,
.I wsys
shows the end of what was written to its
.B cons
(and
.BR text )
and the line being typed, wrapped to the window, in the font given by
.BR -f ,
else
.BR $font ,
else
.BR /lib/font/bit/vga/unicode.font
.RI ( font (6));
the wheel scrolls it, output scrolls it back to the end; a tab is
.B $tabstop
(default 4) zeros wide.
As in
.IR rio ,
a scroll bar is at its left: grey, white for the part shown; button 1 in it
scrolls back and button 3 on by as many lines as the mouse is below its top
(again and again while held), button 2 goes to that place of the text.
Button 1 selects text (on a pale background); twice at one place within half
a second, a word, or what is between brackets or quotes when next to one,
or a line when at its start or end; button 2's menu
.B cut
(the selection to the snarf buffer, out of the text),
.BR paste
(the snarf buffer typed; over a selection in the line typed, in its place),
.BR snarf ,
.B plumb
(the selection, else the word around it with
.BR click= ,
to the plumber of
.IR wsys 's
name space, as
.IR rio ),
.B look
(the next place with the selected text),
.B send
(the selection, else the snarf buffer, typed as a line) and
.B scroll
or
.B noscroll
(as
.IR rio 's:
whether output goes to the end while the window shows text further back;
off at the start, there the text shown stays; also
.B scroll
and
.B noscroll
on the window's
.BR wctl ).
Esc toggles
.IR rio 's
hold mode (also
.B holdon
and
.B holdoff
on
.BR consctl ):
lines typed wait until it ends, the text and border are blue; Del ends it and
interrupts.
The snarf buffer is the
.B snarf
file's; when
.I wsys
runs in a window of
.IR rio ,
.IR rio 's.
.PP
.TP
.B stack
Reading gives the windows, bottom to top, a line each:
.I n
.I x0 y0 x1 y1
(its frame on the screen, border and all)
.I border
(its width)
.I hidden
(1 if hidden).
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a window; the open file is then the window's
.BR ctl ,
and the window lives until it is closed.
The window is off the screen, without the focus, until its first
.B present
(or
.BR top ):
then it comes up where the client put it, with its first picture, and
takes the focus (as Wayland's compositors map a surface with its first
buffer; nothing white flashes where it would be made).
Ranges of closed windows are given again.
.TP
.B screen
an
.IR image (6)
of where
.I wsys
draws (as
.IR rio 's;
its header has the rectangle), with what the overlay plane shows over it
(a window's buffer in VRAM through gpu(4)'s
.BR readback ).
.TP
.IB n /cons ", " consctl ", " cursor ", " kbd ", " label ", " mouse ", " screen ", " snarf ", " text ", " wctl ", " wdir ", " window ", " winid ", " winname
as in
.IR rio (4):
the mouse while it is over the window or a button is held (first an
.B r
message after a change of the window), keys as
.IR kbd (3)
messages to
.B kbd
while it is open, else their runes to
.B cons
(a line at a time unless raw: backspace, ^U, ^D, Del);
.B wctl
reads as
.IR rio 's,
a second read waits for a change; writes to
.B cons
collect in
.BR text ;
.B window
and
.B screen
are
.IR image (6)s
of the window (border and all) and of the screen, taken at a read at offset 0
.RI ( mc (1)
takes its width from
.BR window 's
header).
.TP
.IB n /ctl
Reading gives one line of fields:
.I n
.I x0 y0 x1 y1
(the window's content on the screen)
.I w h
.I base size
(the range for its buffers)
.I focus
(1 if it has the focus); Mesa's plan9 WSI knows a window of
.I wsys
by these ten fields.
Messages written:
.RS
.TP
.BI rect " x0 y0 x1 y1
moves the window; with a new size it keeps showing its picture (white
beyond it) until a buffer is presented.
.TP
.BI buffer " b seg w h stride \fR[\fPoff \fR[\fPchan\fR]]\fP
announces buffer
.I b
(0 to 31): segment
.I seg
of
.I w
by
.I h
pixels,
.I off
bytes into it (default 0);
.I chan
is
.B x8r8g8b8
(the default: opaque) or
.B a8r8g8b8
(premultiplied alpha, which counts in
.BR layers ).
.I Wsys
attaches it; it must lie in the window's range, or in the region
where the kernel puts segments made with an address of 0
.RI ( segment (3)):
their addresses are unique anyway.
Several buffers, of one window or more, may be in one segment (a
Wayland client's pool, see
.IR wlsys (4));
it is attached once.
To reuse an address, unbuffer first.
.TP
.BI gpubuffer " b token w h stride \fR[\fPchan\fR]\fP
announces buffer
.I b
as a GPU buffer the client exported from its context of
.IR gpu (4)
.RB ( export ,
which gives
.IR token ).
.I Wsys
imports it into a context of its own on the first device
.RB ( /dev/gpu/0 )
and reads the pixels where the processor sees them (the device's host
visible memory).
Mesa's plan9 WSI does this for Vulkan programs in a window of
.IR wsys :
their frames are not copied by the program.
With
.IR aux/amdgpu ,
a window on top, wholly on the screen, with nothing of
.I wsys
over it (a menu, a sweep) and a GPU buffer as big as its contents (rows
a multiple of 256 bytes) goes on an overlay plane
.RB ( overlay
in
.IR gpu (4)):
the display shows the buffer, copied into VRAM by the GPU, and
.I wsys
copies nothing; a present returns at the display's next vblank, done
by a thread of its own, so that
.I wsys
answers its other clients meanwhile.
When the window may no longer be there, the plane goes off and the last
presented buffer is composed by the GPU again, or copied into the window
by the processor.
To a window with GPU buffers
.I wsys
sends the event
.B overlay 1
when it may go on the plane and
.B overlay 0
when no longer: Mesa's plan9 WSI then makes its swapchain's buffers in
VRAM, which the plane shows as they are (no copy at all), or again where
.I wsys
reads them; a buffer in VRAM the processor cannot read the GPU composes all the same;
copying, it shows nothing new until the client's next buffers come.
.B -O
keeps every window on
.IR wsys 's
screen (tests that read the pixels back);
.B WSYSDEBUG=overlay
writes why a window's present does not go on the plane,
.B WSYSDEBUG=release
each frame composed on the GPU and each buffer given back.
.TP
.BI unbuffer " b
.I wsys
detaches buffer
.I b
(a GPU buffer: lets go of it).
.TP
.BR present " IbP [Ix0 y0 x1 y1P]"
the pixels of the rectangle (in the buffer's coordinates, which are the
window's; all of the buffer that fits without one) go to the screen.
When
.I wsys
is done with the buffer it sends the event
.BI b " bR:"
the client may write it again.
With two or more buffers the client draws the next picture meanwhile.
A buffer the display shows (composed on the GPU,
or on the overlay plane) stays
.IR wsys 's
until another one of the window is shown, at the display's rate, also
while the window is hidden; Mesa's plan9 WSI gives the program an image
again only on its
.BR b ,
so that Vulkan programs draw no faster than the display shows (an image
not back within 2 seconds it takes back, with a message); its swapchains
have three images then (one shown, one on its way, one being drawn).
.TP
.BR layers " [" + "]\fIb x y\fR ..."
the window shows these buffers, bottom to top, each with its top left
corner at
.I x y
in the window (a Wayland window with subsurfaces,
.IR wlsys (4)):
the bottom one is copied, one over others blended if it has alpha
.RB ( a8r8g8b8 ;
Plan 9's and Wayland's premultiplied over).
.B +
marks a buffer with new content: it gets its
.B s
when a frame shows it.
A listed buffer stays
.IR wsys 's
while listed; one no longer listed comes back with
.B b
once no frame shows it (a
.B present
ends the layers: all but its buffer come back).
When the GPU composes the screen and has all of them it composes them
(a layer with alpha drawn over the frame by a shader that reads the
client's buffer itself); else
.IR draw (3)
does, through an image of
.B a8r8g8b8
.RB ( SoverD ).
A window with layers does not go on the overlay plane.
.B WSYSDEBUG=layers
writes which composes a window's layers.
.TP
.B top
raises the window and gives it the focus.
.TP
.BR popup " [" grab ]
before the first
.BR present :
the window has no border (a menu's or a tooltip's,
.IR wlsys (4));
shown, it takes the focus with
.B grab
(a menu), else leaves it where it is (a tooltip).
.TP
.BI label " text
.TP
.B delete
.RE
.TP
.IB n /event
Reading blocks until there are events; each is a line:
.BI m " x y buttons msec
(the mouse, in the window's coordinates, while it is over the window or a button is held,
and once when it has left it),
.BI k " rune
(a key, the rune in hexadecimal),
.BI r " w h
(a new size),
.BI f " focus
(1: the window got the focus, 0: lost it),
.BI s " b
(buffer
.I b
is on the display, composed, on the overlay plane or copied: the time for
the next picture, as Wayland's frame callbacks;
.I wsys
may keep it until another one is shown),
.BI b " b
(buffer
.I b
released),
.BI overlay " 0|1
(whether its buffers may go on the overlay plane).
Programs skip events they do not know.
.PP
.I Wsys
composes on the processor through
.IR draw (3):
a background, the windows bottom to top, each with a border (blue with the focus).
Mouse events come from
.B /dev/mouse
(opened once: the kernel's takes one opener),
keys from
.B /dev/kbd
(else
.B /dev/cons
in raw mode);
a button pressed over a window gives it the focus and raises it;
on its border, as in
.IR rio ,
button 1 (or 2) sweeps it to a new size (the corner when within 20 pixels of
it, else the edge; an outline until the button comes up, then the client gets
an
.B r
event) and button 3 moves the window.
Button 3 on the background or in a text window gives
.IR rio 's
menu:
.B New
(sweep a rectangle with button 3: a window with
.B rc
in it),
.B Resize
(sweep from a point in the window to its new corner),
.BR Move ,
.B Delete
and
.B Hide
(button 3 on the window), and the hidden windows by their labels, to bring one
back; a cross is the cursor while a command waits for its button.
A hidden window is off the screen, as in
.IR rio ;
.B wctl
says
.B hidden
and takes
.B hide
and
.BR unhide .
Keys go to the focused window.
With
.BR -x ,
.I wsys
prints the name it posted and its rectangle (for scripts); otherwise nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
In
.IR plan9.ini ,
.B desktop=wsys
makes the terminal's profile start
.B wsys -i riostart
instead of
.IR rio .
.B wsysflags=-g
adds
.B -g
(and logs what wsys says to
.B wsysboot.log
on the boot stick's ESP);
.B comprun
in one of its windows checks the composition (two
.B vktri
at once, one partly covered) and logs to
.B comp.log
on the boot stick's ESP.
.PP
A client in C: see
.BR /tests/ui/wsystest.c .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/wsys
(Rust;
.BR scripts/build/cargo.sh )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rio (1),
.IR rio (4),
.IR wlsys (4),
.IR window (1),
.IR draw (3),
.IR segment (3)
.SH BUGS
.I Wsys
must be allowed to attach the clients' segments: the same user, or the
host owner.
With the processor backend a buffer is released as soon as it is
copied.
When
.I wsys
exits the windows are gone; clients cannot announce theirs again yet.
GPU buffers: those of
.I virtiogpu
(venus) and
.I amdgpu
(RADV) and
.I i915
(hasvk) so far; when the processor composes,
.I wsys
copies them to the screen.
On the GPU, draw(3)'s changes are still copied by the processor (into a buffer the
GPU reads), and a client presenting the buffer shown again gets it back
at once, while it is still read each frame.
Programs that read the screen through
.IR draw (3)
(rather than
.BR /mnt/wsys/screen )
do not see the GPU windows, and a cursor drawn by
.IR draw (3)
(vga hwgc soft) is hidden by them; the GPU drivers' cursor plane is not.
Each buffer is a segment of
.IR wsys 's
own as well; a process has room for some 250
(of which Rust's threads take 64), so all windows together get about 190 buffers.
.I Rio
in a window breaks when the window goes (its procs, on the note group's
.BR hangup ,
as in a window of
.IR rio ).
